    "case_id": "584",
    "case_summary": "A 60-year-old man with a history of squamous cell cancer of the right tongue and cirrhosis from hepatitis C. His platelet count 5 days before surgery was 115,000/µL. Patient had undergone right glossectomy with bilateral neck dissection. Medical history includes hepatitis C with a nucleic acid amplification test result of 1679 IU/mL. Patient developed gastrointestinal bleeding with two episodes of frank hematochezia.\n\nNo specific physical examination findings were detailed in the case report.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:    * Platelet count: 2,000/µL    * Prothrombin time: Normal    * Activated partial thromboplastin time: Normal    * HIV status: Negative    * Heparin-associated antibody: Negative    * Bone marrow biopsy was performed  - Imaging Studies:    * Computed Tomography:      Title: CT of the abdomen      Description: Shows cirrhosis of the liver        * Visceral Angiogram:      Title: Angiogram findings      Description: No active extravasation seen        * Post-embolization Imaging:      Title: Axial and coronal contrast-enhanced CT images      Description: Shows approximately 60% infarction of the spleen (obtained 10 days after splenic embolization)",
